### Negative controls

**Control 1 (hook wiring) — and a correction.** The plan specified breaking the
`refs/etude/*` exemption branch. Run as specified, the suite still passed 18/18.
That is not a wiring failure; the control was badly chosen. With `refs/etude/*`
removed from the exempt case, such a ref falls to the `*)` branch, which sets
`_all_exempt=false` but leaves `_has_code_ref` false — and the next guard exits 0
when there is no code ref. B1's assertions (exit 0, audit not called) hold
either way, so the breakage is masked downstream.

Re-run against a branch whose breakage is not masked — deleting
`_has_code_ref=true` from the `refs/heads/*` case — the suite fails **5 of 18,
exit 1**. That is the real proof `$PRE_PUSH_HOOK` resolves to the live hook.

**Control 2 (bd shim) — inconclusive as specified, so it was strengthened.**
Removing the `export PATH` line left 18/18 green, because this machine's real
`bd` happens to behave compatibly. The plan already anticipated this and rested
the guarantee on inspection, which passes.

To show the shim is load-bearing rather than decorative, the shim was replaced
with a `bd` that exits 7: **13 of 18 cases fail.** So the suite genuinely
depends on the shim to be insulated from whatever `bd` a machine has; it simply
cannot be demonstrated by removal on a machine whose real `bd` is compatible.

All three mutations were reverted and both files confirmed byte-identical to
their pre-control state.

